{
  "dom_id": "step-q105709",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}-4x+4=0,$ which corresponds to $x=2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,1]$ is allowed. The intervals $(-2,1]$ and $[-1,2)$ are not closed, while $[0,2]$ and $[-1,3]$ contain the point $x=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
